<p>My <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AppleCare%2B">AppleCare</a> informed me that my Apple Watch&rsquo;s coverage is running out. Yep, turns out it&rsquo;s been <a href="https://taonaw.com/2024/04/14/so-i-got.html">two years since I got it</a>. I&rsquo;ve been wearing it every day pretty much since, so I thought I&rsquo;d write up a follow-up to my initial post and see how it lives up to the hype.</p>
<p>Since I got the Apple Watch, I stopped using <a href="https://www.trainwell.com/">TrainWell</a> (which at the time was called Co-Pilot, until Microsoft came knocking on their door). Overall, I think leaving Trainwell was a good choice, even though it&rsquo;s a good app. If you&rsquo;re curious, <a href="https://taonaw.com/2025/02/02/the-woes-of-flexiblity-vs.html">I expanded on this previously</a>. The Apple Watch is still good for exercise, and I use it often for walking, running, and strength training. Of all of those, I think it really shines for jogging. I can use it without my phone (so I don&rsquo;t run with a slab in a pouch or around my arm), and it syncs automatically when I&rsquo;m back. It gives me a map of my route and my speeds in various colors, so I know where I stopped to take a breath and how long the entire thing was, along with a bunch of other metrics for average speed, elevation, etc.</p>
<p>Strength training is&hellip; OK. It doesn&rsquo;t do much besides measuring how long I&rsquo;ve been training. There&rsquo;s some useful data, like my heart rate or calories burned, but I&rsquo;m not generally concerned with those. What I am missing is a way for it to track the exercises and sets I&rsquo;m doing (TrainWell did this nicely), and maybe resume automatically after each set, but I can live without it. I currently track my exercises in Apple Notes because I can see them on the watch (took Apple long enough): this way I can take a quick look and see what I need to do without looking at my phone, which can be distracting.</p>
<p>Meditation is treated like other exercises on the watch, under &ldquo;mind and body.&rdquo; That&rsquo;s too little in my opinion, and shouldn&rsquo;t be included as an exercise cateogry, but I guess that&rsquo;s why there are dedicated meditation apps. When I time my meditation with the watch this way, the slight vibration works as a way to tell me how far along I am, but even that can be too distracting, and I&rsquo;m considering just using a soft audible chime from my phone, using a regular countdown. I recently started using a new meditation app, <a href="https://www.wakingup.com/">Waking Up</a>, after a friend recommended it. It&rsquo;s too early to expand on it here, but so far, it&rsquo;s pretty good - <del>no Apple watch app though</del>. Actually, there is an App, I didn&rsquo;t realize. I&rsquo;m not sure it does more than just measuring how long I meditate, though. I shall explore.</p>
<p>My sleeping issues&hellip; Oh man. What sleep.<br>
The Apple Watch doesn&rsquo;t <em>help</em> me with sleep as much as it reinforces what I already know: I don&rsquo;t sleep enough. The new sleep grade from Apple <a href="https://support.apple.com/guide/watch/view-your-sleep-score-apded441a669/26/watchos/26">makes sense</a>, and it&rsquo;s explained well enough, but it&rsquo;s like being reminded constantly that I suck at math all over again, as was the case for me during my school and college years.  I <em>know</em> I have a problem, and constantly looking at graphs and charts and bad grades visualizing this to me every day is not helpful, though I&rsquo;m aware it&rsquo;s up to me to do something else with the information or just turn it off completely. The positive side of it is that it helps me to justify naps during the day, as I do need them.</p>
<p>As for the gimmicks, like taking selfies with the watch or the double-tap to open and dismiss certain apps - I don&rsquo;t use those. The watch face is too small, and the interface is often too minimal to do much with. Every now and then I use it to respond to a message in WhatsApp, which I use with the family, but when I do, I just tap the microphone to say something quick. Otherwise I reach for my phone, which I have on me all the time anyway.</p>
<p>I got rid of Teams on the watch long ago, even though I thought it would be useful for work. The number of messages I get on this app is impossible to manage, and I find that I mute conversations constantly. The last thing I need is another vibration on my wrist to remind me that two people are talking to each other in yet another 20-plus person chat which happens to include me.</p>
<p>There is one gimmicky feature I use all the time though, and that&rsquo;s the reminders. Having the watch on my wrist is the quickest and easiest way for me to remind myself to do something later. Even if I mess up the dictation, which happens often enough, I have it written down and it&rsquo;s easy to go back and edit later on my phone or Mac.</p>
<p>Overall, looking at what I wrote two years ago, the hype died down for sure. I still find that I don&rsquo;t like how the Apple watch <em>looks</em>. It&rsquo;s a generic square slab, and I miss how my Casio G-Shock looked on my wrist. Still, it&rsquo;s a piece of technology I use every day, mostly for MFA for work when I sign into various apps, and when I pay for stuff (being able to pay for the subway this way, even through the sleeve of my coat in the winter, is very nice). One feature I didn&rsquo;t think much about when I got it was how well it works with Apple Maps: when I walk, it vibrats e a certain way for a right turn than it does for a left turn, and it&rsquo;s really nice to be able to keep my hands in my pocket (again, this comes in handy in the winter).</p>
<p>I&rsquo;m probably going to buy a new one soon, though I feel like if I wait another year or so it&rsquo;s not a big deal.</p>
